Slopes (first-quarter mean to last-quarter mean, about 90 minutes apart):
- api-task-lifecycle: RSS +871 KB (+0.98%); first sample to last +7308 KB, mostly within the first quarter, about 30 minutes (warm-up: first sample < Q1 mean); threads 8→9; same PID throughout (no restart)
- api-chat: RSS +521 KB (+0.58%); first sample to last +6764 KB, mostly within the first quarter, about 30 minutes (warm-up: first sample < Q1 mean); threads 8→8; same PID throughout (no restart)
- api-sse: RSS +524 KB (+0.65%); first sample to last +1108 KB, mostly within the first quarter, about 30 minutes (warm-up: first sample < Q1 mean); threads 7→8; same PID throughout (no restart)
- fds: NOT MEASURED. The sidecar read 0 open fds in every sample for all three services. A live process cannot have 0 fds, so the reading failed (the `ls /proc/<pid>/fd` read is unreliable in this sandbox). fd stability is NOT claimed.
- The harness has NO in-tool leak metric. The RSS/threads slopes above are external observations over 2h at baseline rate. They do not prove the absence of a leak at higher load or over longer durations.

## §4 Classification for R-177 (soak row)
- measured + pass (2h, baseline rate, volatile, same-VM generator): api-task-lifecycle, api-chat, api-sse
- not_measured (NOT pass): api-health, api-auth, api-agents, api-admin-tools, api-memory, api-tasks-submit, api-tasks-read, frontend — 8 services
- Not covered by this soak: durable storage, multi-worker, production host, separate generator host, loads above the baseline rate.

